      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the cave analogy. Let's dive into the science.\n\nThe protein content in your flour blend can affect the enzymatic activity of the microorganisms in your starter. Proteins like gliadin and glutenin in wheat flour can inhibit the activity of certain enzymes, such as amylase and protease, which are essential for the breakdown of starches and proteins. This can lead to a slower breakdown of nutrients, which can, in turn, affect the balance of lactobacillus and yeast populations.\n\nFor example, gliadin can bind to the yeast's cell surface, reducing its ability to ferment sugars. On the other hand, the lactobacillus can produce enzymes that break down gliadin, but this process can be slowed down if the protein content is too high.\n\nIn terms of pH stability, the protein content can also affect the buffering capacity of the starter. Proteins like casein and whey proteins can bind to hydrogen ions, helping to stabilize the pH. However, if the protein content is too high, it can also lead to an over-buffering effect, which can cause the pH to drop more rapidly.\n\nTo stabilize the microbial activity and pH, you might consider adjusting the protein content of your",
